Architecture: Proxmox VE, ZFS, PCIe, NUMA, and Cluster Design
1. Objective
This document focuses on the core mechanisms that make a real Proxmox environment stable and performant:

ZFS design
ARC / L2ARC / SLOG
PCIe bifurcation
NUMA topology
cluster orchestration
storage and backup design
security model
2. Proxmox VE as a technical synthesis
Proxmox VE is not just a hypervisor UI. It is a complete platform:

Debian Linux base
KVM for virtualization
LXC for lightweight containers
ZFS for storage
Corosync + pmxcfs for cluster communication
API-driven automation and IaC
3. ZFS design
ZFS provides:

copy-on-write
checksums
scrubbing
instant snapshots
replication
compression
deduplication
integrity-first storage design
ARC, L2ARC, SLOG
ARC: RAM cache
L2ARC: SSD/NVMe read cache
SLOG/ZIL: sync-write logging device
4. NUMA and host performance
NUMA is crucial on multi-socket enterprise hardware:

pin vCPUs
align memory locality
map storage and NICs near CPU domains
5. PCIe and VFIO
GPU passthrough requires:

IOMMU enabled
correct BIOS settings
verified IOMMU groups
no host driver conflicts
6. Proxmox cluster
Use:

Corosync
pmxcfs
fencing
quorum awareness
recovery path validation
7. Backup strategy
Use PBS:

incremental backups
deduplication
encryption
restore testing EOF
cat > docs/why-enterprise-hardware.md <<'EOF'

Why Enterprise Hardware Endures
1. Quality by design
Enterprise hardware is built for:

24/7 operation
thermal resilience
redundant power
ECC memory
higher I/O throughput
long product support windows
2. MTBF and manufacturing quality
Enterprise hardware is designed for reliability and continuity, not just convenience.

3. ECC RAM
ECC protects against:

single-bit errors
silent data corruption
memory faults in large systems
4. TCO logic
Second-life enterprise hardware is often more affordable per core and per GB RAM than consumer hardware.

5. Cloud trap
Cloud gives convenience but often creates:

price escalation
lock-in
egress costs
reduced control
Bare metal + Proxmox gives ownership and control. EOF

